It started at Harvard, on the fourth of February, two thousand **four**.
Mark Zuckerberg and Eduardo Saverin each put in a thousand **dollars**.

// CARD a1-02 · SEE: the real Facebook like sign by the road; on "year" a pencil writes $200,000 next to the word facebook and rings it · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@price_pencil;pic:meta_facebook_like_sign_full;word:$200,000;go:year;x:.6;y:.36;lab:FACEBOOK.COM · 2005;src:the domain alone;out:right"
A year later, the company paid two hundred thousand dollars just for the name facebook dot **com**. (J)

// CARD a1-03 · SEE: real trading screens, dim; a black LED board hangs over them; on "May" FB · IPO clicks up and MAY 2012 · ABOUT $16 BILLION RAISED lights word by word · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@led_quote;bg:stock_finance_exchange_floor_05;head:FB · IPO;q:MAY 2012 · ABOUT $16 BILLION RAISED;go:May;span:2;by:Facebook goes public;in:right"
In May twenty twelve, Facebook went public and raised about sixteen billion **dollars**.

// CARD a1-04 · SEE: two solid blocks rise out of a dark floor one after the other: INSTAGRAM · 2012 $1B, then WHATSAPP · 2014 $19B, nineteen times taller · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@blocks;vals:1,19;labs:INSTAGRAM · 2012,WHATSAPP · 2014;txts:$1B,$19B;go:Instagram;gap:2.2;note:13 employees and 55 employees;out:right"
A month earlier, it had bought Instagram for about one billion. Instagram had thirty million users and thirteen **employees**. (J)
Two years later, it paid about nineteen billion dollars for WhatsApp, which had four hundred and fifty million users and fifty-five **employees**.

// CARD a1-05 · SEE: a closed ring of stations over real phone users: USERS → ATTENTION → ADVERTISERS → MONEY; arrows draw on their words and the ring closes in gold · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@money_loop;bg:meta_phone_walkers_hong_kong;nodes:USERS@users~DATA@knew~ADVERTISERS@advertisers~MONEY@worth;in:right"
The users never paid anything. The advertisers paid for the **users**.
And the more Facebook knew about each person, the more each advert was **worth**.

// CARD a1-06 · SEE: NEW: one red dot in the middle, YOU INSTALL; on "installed" gold lines shoot out to a ring of friends that light one by one; on "friends" fainter lines reach their friends · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@friend_fan;bg:stock_phone_social_feed_09;n:14;go:installed;go2:friends;ulab:YOU INSTALL;lab:ONE INSTALL;sub:IT COULD READ YOUR FRIENDS;out:right"
In those years, an app on Facebook could read more than the person who installed it. It could read their **friends**.

// CARD a1-07 · SEE: the real FTC building in Washington; on "promise" a placard is planted in front of it: 2012 · A PROMISE TO THE FTC · SEAM in right
# cine act=setup fx="code:@sign_on_footage;clip:meta_ftc_apex_building_front;sign:A PROMISE;sub:to the FTC · 2012;go:promise;x:.5;y:.42;in:right"
That same year, twenty twelve, Facebook had made a promise to the Federal Trade Commission. It would not mislead users about their **privacy**.
Remember that promise. It comes back with a price **tag**.

// CARD a1-08 · SEE: MOTIF: the bill feeds out; on "costing" it prints FTC ORDER · 2012: A PROMISE under the $25 billion line; IT STARTS WITH ONE PERMISSION · SEAM out right
# cine act=lesson fx="code:@bill_tape;bg:meta_el_texture_server_bokeh;lines:NEW MEXICO · 21 OCT 2026^$25,000,000,000~FTC ORDER · 2012^A PROMISE;new:1;go:costing;lab:IT STARTS WITH;sub:one permission;out:right"
So how does one personality quiz end up costing twenty-five billion dollars? It starts with that one **permission**.

